      paragraphs: [
        `The techniques above only matter if something actually produces the files, every time, without anyone remembering. The failure mode is a beautifully optimised launch followed by eighteen months of studio staff uploading originals straight from the photographer, until the site is as heavy as the one it replaced.`,
        `There are three workable answers. Build-time processing, where the site generator takes the original and produces every format and width automatically when the site is built — the cleanest option, with no ongoing service cost, and our default for a studio publishing a few projects a month. An image delivery service that transforms on request from a single original, which suits studios publishing constantly or holding very large back catalogues, at a recurring cost. Or manual export by whoever adds the project, which works exactly as long as the person who was trained is still there.`,
        `Whichever route, the content management system needs guardrails rather than good intentions. Our defaults: reject an upload above a size threshold with a message that explains what to do, transform automatically rather than relying on anyone to export correctly, require alt text before a project can be published, and warn when an image's aspect ratio does not match the slot it is going into. Guardrails are unglamorous and they are the reason the site is still fast in year two.`,
        `There is also a one-off decision about the back catalogue. A studio with three hundred past projects does not need all of them online — twenty-five well-presented projects convert better than three hundred thinly presented ones, and loading three hundred is a substantial data-entry cost whoever does it. We usually recommend launching with a curated set and adding selectively, and we will quote bulk loading separately rather than burying it in the build price.`,
      ],
